<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<ui version="4.0">
 <class>ExtendedSliderUi</class>
 <widget class="QWidget" name="ExtendedSliderUi">
  <property name="geometry">
   <rect>
    <x>0</x>
    <y>0</y>
    <width>519</width>
    <height>138</height>
   </rect>
  </property>
  <property name="windowTitle">
   <string>Form</string>
  </property>
  <!--
    Eighteen of these stack up in the tool dock (three in Raise/Lower alone), so the layout
    metrics here are what decide how much of the panel is chrome. Four things were costing
    height or legibility for nothing.

    First, every layout took the Fusion defaults (9px margins, 6px spacing). A control that is
    already inside a group box, itself inside a panel, does not need a third 9px inset; the
    parent supplies the gutter. Margins are zeroed and the row gap is set explicitly.

    Second, the slider and the pressure bar each sat in their own single item QHBoxLayout.
    Those wrappers were pure overhead, and the pressureBar one was actively harmful: the bar is
    hidden unless a tablet is driving the value (see ExtendedSlider.cpp), and while Qt skips a
    hidden WIDGET in a layout, a nested LAYOUT holding it is not empty, so it kept claiming its
    margins and spacing on every one of the eighteen instances. Both widgets are now direct
    children of the vertical layout, which makes the hidden bar genuinely free.

    Third, the slider took whatever height the style's slider thickness happened to be - around
    22px under Fusion, and none of it used. The band is pinned as a MINIMUM as well as a maximum,
    so the row height is this file's decision rather than the style's. That was the single
    biggest saving on the panel, before the row gap below dropped from 3 to 2 and then, in this
    pass, from 2 to 0.

    THE BAND IS 20px AND IT IS DERIVED, NOT CHOSEN. It is Design::SLIDER_BAND, and the whole
    derivation is on that constant in DesignTokens.hpp; in one line, QSS width/height size the
    CONTENT rect and the border adds, QStyleSheetStyle places the handle inside the groove's
    contents rect expanded by the handle's negative margins, and the WIDGET band then clips it:

        handle box = declared QSS size + 2 x border width
        handle box = groove box + 2 x |cross-axis margin|
        grabbable  = min(handle box, band), centred

    The theme draws a 6px groove with the handle margin at -7px, so 6 + 7 + 7 = 20 is the box
    and a band under 20 clips the grip into a circle with a flat top and bottom.

    BOTH NUMBERS BELOW HAVE TO MOVE TOGETHER. A maximumHeight clamps the widget below any QSS
    min-height, so changing only the minimum leaves the grip clipped to the maximum, and changing
    only the maximum leaves the layout free to give it less.

    THIS IS THE CHANGE AN EARLIER PASS MADE AND THEN REVERTED, AND THE REASON TO REDO IT IS NOT
    THE REASON IT WAS MADE. That pass widened the band while the sheet's handle box stayed at
    16, and it argued that the band sets the hit target because Qt derives SC_SliderHandle from
    the WIDGET height. That argument is true of an UNSTYLED slider and false of this one, and
    the revert measured it with a standalone Qt 5.15.2 probe that loads the shipped sheet, puts
    a 212px slider at 50 percent and sweeps the pointer pixel by pixel counting the positions
    QStyle::hitTestComplexControl(CC_Slider, ...) reports as SC_SliderHandle - the exact call
    QSlider::mousePressEvent uses to choose between dragging and page-stepping:

      sheet                    band   handle rect       pixels that GRAB the handle
      6px tick, 4px groove      16     6x16 @(103,0)     96 =  6 x 16
      16px grip, 6px groove     14    16x16 @(98,-1)    224 = 16 x 14  (grip clipped by band)
      16px grip, 6px groove     16    16x16 @(98,0)     256 = 16 x 16
      16px grip, 6px groove     18    16x16 @(98,1)     256 = 16 x 16
      16px grip, 6px groove     20    16x16 @(98,2)     256 = 16 x 16
      16px grip, 6px groove     22    16x16 @(98,3)     256 = 16 x 16
      none, QWindowsVistaStyle  16    16x16 @(98,0)     256 = 16 x 16
      none, QWindowsVistaStyle  20    16x20 @(98,0)     320 = 16 x 20

    Read the middle four rows again: with a sheet loaded the handle rect is 16x16 at EVERY band
    from 16 up, because QStyleSheetStyle sizes it from the QSS box and centres it. So widening
    the band alone bought exactly zero grabbable pixels and charged 4px x 18 instances for it -
    which is why it was reverted, correctly.

    What is different now is that the BOX moves with the band: the sheet's grip goes to a 20px
    box in the same pass, and by the same table a 20px box in a 20px band is 20 x 20 = 400
    grabbable pixels against the 256 those rows all report. The cost is unchanged and honest -
    +4px x 18 instances, +12px of dock height in the densest panel, Raise/Lower with three of
    these - but this time it buys 56 percent more hit target on the control that sets brush
    radius and speed, instead of nothing.

    THE CHAIN IS NOW READ OUT OF QT RATHER THAN INFERRED FROM A PROBE, because the probe gave
    the answer without giving the reason, and the reason is what tells a later edit whether it
    has broken anything. QStyleSheetStyle::subControlRect, CC_Slider (qstylesheetstyle.cpp:
    5631-5660, Qt 5.9.9 sources; the function is unchanged in 5.15):

        gr        = positionRect(groove rule)                6px tall, CENTRED in the band
        cr        = grooveRule.contentsRect(gr)              still 6 - no border, no padding
        len       = handleRule.size().width()                boxSize(16) = 16 + 2 + 2 = 20
        thickness = cr.height()                              6
        cr        = QRect(x + sliderPos, cr.y(), len, thickness)
        return      handleRule.borderRect(cr)

    and borderRect (:1049-1055) SUBTRACTS the margins, so -7px top and bottom adds fourteen:
    6 + 7 + 7 = 20. The rect QSlider::mousePressEvent hit-tests is therefore 20 x 20 = 400px, a
    20px acquisition target on a 6px track, and the band is pinned to exactly 20 below so none
    of it is clipped. At band 18 the disc loses a row top and bottom; at band 22 nothing is
    gained, because the box comes from the sheet and the band only clips it - which is what the
    eight-row table above records from the other direction.

    NOTE FOR ANYONE ADDING A SUB-CONTROL RULE: in that chain the handle is derived from the
    GROOVE rule and from nothing else. ExtendedSlider.cpp gives ::add-page a 1px border so that
    the unfilled part of the track exists at all, and that cannot move the grip, change the band
    or change this file's two 20s - add-page is only ever PAINTED, into a rect built from gr and
    the handle centre (:3260-3276). A border on ::groove would be a different matter, and would
    also be invisible: sub-page and add-page are drawn AFTER the groove over its full box, so
    they paint over its border.

    Fourth, the value was left-aligned, so in a column of three sliders the digits started at
    three different x positions and the eye had nothing to scan down. The spinbox is now
    right-aligned. That is the whole fix and it costs no width: the row is label, expanding
    spacer, spinbox, fixed-width button, so the RIGHT edge of every spinbox in a column is
    already flush regardless of how wide each spinbox is, and right-aligned text puts the digits
    on that shared edge. A minimum width was tried here first and does not work - the spinbox
    size hints are 89px at max 1000, 95px at max 10000 and 71px at max 1, so any floor small
    enough to fit the 250px dock is below the widest of them and aligns nothing. Alignment does
    not touch the range, the step, the decimals or the emitted value.

    The pencil button is the tablet-pressure menu, NOT a mask or curve picker - read
    ExtendedSlider.cpp, the popup it opens holds "Use Tablet" and a sensitivity slider. It had
    no tooltip at all, which is most of why it reads as mysterious. It is now flat (the theme's
    QPushButton:flat is transparent until hovered) so it stops carrying the same visual weight
    as the number beside it, and 20px rather than 22px - which is the slider band exactly, so the
    two rows of this widget agree on one height instead of the button setting the taller one.

    No widget was renamed, removed or reordered; ExtendedSlider.cpp addresses the same five
    objects and none of its ranges or connections changed.

    ============================================================================================
    FIFTH, AND IT IS THE ONE THAT MADE THE TRACK LOOK DETACHED: THE ROW GROUPED THE WRONG WAY.

    A slider row is two lines that are ONE control, stacked inside a section that separates
    SIBLING controls by S3 = 8px (ToolWidgetStyle.hpp, sectionColumn). For the two lines to read
    as one thing, the gap inside the row has to be visibly smaller than the gap between rows.
    It was not, and the reason is that a QSlider's 20px band is mostly air:

      QSS width/height size the CONTENT rect, and the theme's groove is 6px. Qt centres it -
      defaultPosition(PseudoElement_SliderGroove) is Qt::AlignCenter and its position mode is
      PositionMode_Absolute (qstylesheetstyle.cpp:2180-2181 and :2308-2311, Qt 5.9.9), so the
      groove occupies y = 7..12 of the 20px band and there are SEVEN TRANSPARENT PIXELS above it
      and seven below.

    So the gap a reader actually sees is not the layout spacing, it is the layout spacing plus
    that dead band. With the spacing at 2:

      value line  ->  visible track      2 + 7  =  9px
      visible track  ->  next row's label   7 + 8  = 15px

    9 against 15 is a ratio of 1.67, which is inside the range where the eye cannot decide which
    line the track belongs to - and "the handle looks detached" is exactly what a 1.67 ratio
    looks like. Dropping the row spacing to 0 makes it 7 against 15, a ratio of 2.14, and the
    two lines bind. It also gives 2px per row back to the dock, which over the twenty-three
    construction sites in this tree is 46px, and 6px in Raise/Lower alone. (Twenty-three,
    counted for this pass: eighteen `new` across eight files, two more in
    ChunkManipulatorPanel, three promoted widgets in BrushStack.ui. This file's opening line
    still says "eighteen of these", which was the count before the chunk manipulator and the
    stamp tool existed.)

    ZERO IS SAFE HERE PRECISELY BECAUSE OF THE DEAD BAND. Nothing touches: the spin box's bottom
    border and the first painted pixel of the track are still 7px apart. That is also true of
    the pressure bar, which is the third item in this layout and sits 7px below the groove for
    the same reason - and it should be tight, because it is a readout OF the value the track
    above it is setting, not a separate control.

    SIXTH, THE VALUE AND ITS MENU ARE ONE UNIT AND NOW SPACE LIKE ONE. The horizontal layout ran
    at 6px, which is not a step on the design system's scale at all - it is the Fusion default
    that was left behind when the margins were zeroed. The gaps it governs are label|spacer,
    spacer|spinbox and spinbox|button; the first two are swallowed by the expanding spacer, so
    the only one anybody sees is the last, between the number and the pencil that configures how
    that number responds to pen pressure. That is a control and its menu, which is S1 = 4px
    ("inside a unit") and not S3. Three gaps at 6 become three at 4, so the row's minimum width
    drops by 6px as well.

    THE ROW STILL FITS THE DOCK FLOOR, and this is the constraint that governs any change here:
    ToolWidgetStyle.hpp pins every tool to TOOL_CONTENT_WIDTH = 250px and a section card spends
    CARD_BOX_WIDTH = 26px of it, so a slider row has 224px to lay out in.

    Four items means THREE inter-item gaps - label|spacer, spacer|spinbox, spinbox|button -
    and the spacer's minimum is 0, so the row's minimum width is
    label + 3 x spacing + spinbox + button. Measured for this pass with GetTextExtentPoint32
    against the shipped Segoe UI at the 11px the sheet gives QLabel#slider-prefix, every prefix
    setPrefix is called with in this tree:

        Angle of Repose:  88      Height offset:  71      Radius:  38      End:      26
        Inner Radius:     68      Inner radius:   65      weight   39      Size:     23
        Strength:         48                              Speed:   35      Start:    30

    Taking the widest label with the widest spin box the file's own note records - 95px, at a
    maximum of 10000 - gives a conservative upper bound, conservative because those two do not
    occur on the same row (the 0..10000 site is ShaderTool's "Speed:", a 35px label):

        spacing 6   88 + 6 + 0 + 6 + 95 + 6 + 20 = 221px    3px of slack in 224
        spacing 4   88 + 4 + 0 + 4 + 95 + 4 + 20 = 215px    9px of slack in 224

    So the row was inside the floor by three pixels and is inside it by nine. NOTHING HERE
    WIDENS THE ROW; both changes make it narrower, so the texturing tool's Paint tab keeps the
    margin it was measured with.
  -->
  <layout class="QVBoxLayout" name="verticalLayout">
   <!-- 0, not 2: the QSlider band already supplies 7px of transparent margin above and below
        the 6px groove, so this is a 7px gap inside the row against a 15px gap between rows.
        See "FIFTH" above for the derivation. -->
   <property name="spacing">
    <number>0</number>
   </property>
   <property name="leftMargin">
    <number>0</number>
   </property>
   <property name="topMargin">
    <number>0</number>
   </property>
   <property name="rightMargin">
    <number>0</number>
   </property>
   <property name="bottomMargin">
    <number>0</number>
   </property>
   <item>
    <layout class="QHBoxLayout" name="horizontalLayout">
     <!-- Design::S1. The only gap a reader sees here is number-to-menu-button, and a control
          and its own menu are one unit. See "SIXTH" above. -->
     <property name="spacing">
      <number>4</number>
     </property>
     <property name="leftMargin">
      <number>0</number>
     </property>
     <property name="topMargin">
      <number>0</number>
     </property>
     <property name="rightMargin">
      <number>0</number>
     </property>
     <property name="bottomMargin">
      <number>0</number>
     </property>
     <item>
      <widget class="QLabel" name="label">
       <property name="text">
        <string>Name:</string>
       </property>
      </widget>
     </item>
     <item>
      <spacer name="horizontalSpacer">
       <property name="orientation">
        <enum>Qt::Horizontal</enum>
       </property>
       <property name="sizeHint" stdset="0">
        <size>
         <width>40</width>
         <height>20</height>
        </size>
       </property>
      </spacer>
     </item>
     <item>
      <widget class="QDoubleSpinBox" name="doubleSpinBox">
       <property name="alignment">
        <set>Qt::AlignRight|Qt::AlignTrailing|Qt::AlignVCenter</set>
       </property>
      </widget>
     </item>
     <item>
      <widget class="QPushButton" name="tabletControlMenuButton">
       <property name="sizePolicy">
        <sizepolicy hsizetype="Fixed" vsizetype="Fixed">
         <horstretch>0</horstretch>
         <verstretch>0</verstretch>
        </sizepolicy>
       </property>
       <property name="minimumSize">
        <size>
         <width>20</width>
         <height>20</height>
        </size>
       </property>
       <property name="maximumSize">
        <size>
         <width>20</width>
         <height>20</height>
        </size>
       </property>
       <property name="text">
        <string/>
       </property>
       <property name="flat">
        <bool>true</bool>
       </property>
      </widget>
     </item>
    </layout>
   </item>
   <item>
    <widget class="QSlider" name="slider">
     <property name="minimumSize">
      <size>
       <width>0</width>
       <height>20</height>
      </size>
     </property>
     <property name="maximumSize">
      <size>
       <width>16777215</width>
       <height>20</height>
      </size>
     </property>
     <property name="orientation">
      <enum>Qt::Horizontal</enum>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item>
    <widget class="QProgressBar" name="pressureBar">
     <property name="maximumSize">
      <size>
       <width>16777215</width>
       <height>4</height>
      </size>
     </property>
     <property name="value">
      <number>0</number>
     </property>
     <property name="textVisible">
      <bool>false</bool>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
  </layout>
 </widget>
 <resources/>
 <connections/>
</ui>
